<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mongolian People's Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1992 CE · capital Ulaanbaatar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The world's second communist state, a Soviet ally for nearly seventy years, which made Mongolia a modern state with recognised independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolian-peoples-party">Mongolian People's Party</a> took power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> help in 1921 under the limited monarchy of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bogd-khanate-of-mongolia">Bogd Khanate of Mongolia</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a> died in 1924, the party abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the Mongolian People's Republic, renaming the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> ('Red Hero').</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The country followed the Soviet model closely. Forced collectivisation provoked a revolt in 1932, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Khorloogiin Choibalsan</a> the purges of 1937 to 1939 killed many thousands of lamas, nobles and officials and destroyed most monasteries. Soviet and Mongolian forces defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-khalkhin-gol">Khalkhin Gol</a> in 1939. The Cyrillic alphabet was adopted in the 1940s, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yumjaagiin-tsedenbal">Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal</a> from 1952 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a> industrialised and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1961.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Inspired by reform in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, peaceful protests in the winter of 1989 to 1990 led to multi-party elections in 1990, and the 1992 constitution created the democratic state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, ending the people's republic. Soviet troops had left by 1992.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mongolian-peoples-republic.jpg" alt="Mongolian People's Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by User:latebird · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Mongolian_People%27s_Republic_(1945%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1992 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ulaanbaatar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khorloogiin Choibalsan, Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal, Jambyn Batmönkh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism, Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khalkha Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mongolia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>MPR, People's Republic of Mongolia</dd></dl></aside></div>
